{"availability":"available","full":"nkbt/component-router","name":"component-router","description":"Redux-based component routing solution","category":"Other","language":"JavaScript","stars":93,"forks":7,"created":"2015-06-18T05:03:05Z","updated":"2021-08-17T14:19:54Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://nkbt.github.io/component-router","topics":[],"demo":"http://nkbt.github.io/component-router","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-16T05:07:07.228Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-16T05:07:07.228Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-16T05:07:07.228Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T05:49:30.034Z","stars":92,"forks":7},{"at":"2026-10-12T07:58:38.995Z","stars":92,"forks":7},{"at":"2026-10-16T05:07:07.228Z","stars":93,"forks":7}],"technologies":["React"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["The idea of partial routing is coming from the need to track state of independent components in the URL.","Commonly used routers are mostly hierarchical. The great example of such a router is react-router. Unfortunately it is not possible to store component's state independently from the other component in a different \"branch\" of hierarchy.","They work perfectly for most of the UIs. But as soon as we are trying to build a complex UI with multiple independent components and each of those has own state you would like to preserve, it becomes a challenging task. ComponentRouter was created to provide a simple way of keeping such state in the URL with query params."],"features":["Chart block, which can be switched from bar to pie","Filter block, that can be opened or closed","Data panel with two tabs: sources and destinations","We can always keep the state of each block, but you will loose that state on page refresh.","We can keep their states in some specialized Redux Store and cache in localStorage or even user settings in database on the server. But we are losing ability to share this page with someone else (unless we have some…"],"sourceUrl":